  "0d8a620b-c9bd-4998-90de-dd06d685c23f": {
   "id": "0d8a620b-c9bd-4998-90de-dd06d685c23f",
   "message": {
    "id": "0d8a620b-c9bd-4998-90de-dd06d685c23f",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1787112674.059,
    "update_time": 1787112677.106572,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "At a high level (confirm specifics with your lawyer/registered agent):\n\n1. **Stated public benefit.** Your certificate of incorporation names a specific public benefit. Directors must balance stockholders' financial interests, the interests of those materially affected, and that benefit.\n2. **Biennial benefit report to stockholders.** Delaware requires a PBC to give stockholders a statement on its promotion of the public benefit at least every two years. It doesn't have to be public or third-party certified unless your charter says so.\n3. **Name / notices.** PBC status must be disclosed (e.g., \"PBC\" in the name, which you have, and in stock certificates/notices).\n4. **Same as any Delaware corp:** annual franchise tax + annual report to the state, due **March 1** each year. Use the assumed par value capital method for franchise tax; the default authorized-shares method can produce a scary-looking bill for startups.\n5. **Investors:** some investors ask about the PBC structure. Most early-stage investors are fine with it, but be ready to explain your benefit statement in one sentence."
     ]
    },
